{"id": "dc-cudnn-nondeterminism-note", "tags": ["done", "caveat", "batch-2"], "expected": false, "why": "GPU drift is expected library behaviour, seeding is done", "state": {"request": "Add a --seed flag so training runs are reproducible.", "hand_off": "`train.py` takes `--seed` and sets it for torch, numpy and every dataloader worker. CPU runs are bit-identical. On GPU, cuDNN's nondeterministic kernels can still move the loss in the fourth decimal; that's expected and documented upstream, not a gap in the seeding."}}
{"id": "dc-active-tab-badge-tradeoff", "tags": ["done", "caveat", "reflex", "batch-2"], "expected": false, "why": "side effect is expected; reload is a routine step", "state": {"request": "Narrow the browser extension's permissions to activeTab.", "hand_off": "The extension now asks only for `activeTab` instead of `<all_urls>`. Expected side effect: the badge count won't refresh on background tabs until you click into one. Reload the extension to pick it up."}}
{"id": "dc-redis-limiter-what-to-look-for", "tags": ["done", "caveat", "reflex", "batch-2"], "expected": false, "why": "restart and what-to-look-for are routine tails", "state": {"request": "Switch the rate limiter to the Redis sliding-window version.", "hand_off": "The rate limiter uses the Redis sliding window now and its tests pass. Restart the API server to load the new config. What to look for: a 429 on the 101st request inside a minute."}}
